If our videos are live streamed to our website (and thus to YouTube), and then the videos are archived with a link on our website, will advertisements still be inserted? (Our current setup uses Vimeo and no ads are inserted).

Moving from Vimeo to YouTube is a common way for churches to save on monthly fees, but you are correct to be concerned about the "user experience." In 2026, YouTube has become much more aggressive with ads, and the rules are different than what you are used to with Vimeo.

Here is the breakdown of how ads will work in your specific setup:

1. Will ads be inserted? Yes, likely. Unlike Vimeo (where you pay a fee to keep the player ad-free), YouTube is a "free" service that pays for its servers by showing ads. The Default: Even if you do not "monetize" your channel (meaning you don't want to earn money from the ads), YouTube reserves the right to show ads on your videos to cover their hosting costs. The "Embedded" Rule: When you put a YouTube video on the St. Mary's website, viewers may see an ad before the video starts (pre-roll) or "overlay" ads (banners) at the bottom.

2. The "Copyright" Trigger (Very Important for Churches) This is the most common reason church videos get ads. If your service includes copyrighted music (hymns, choir performances, or recorded tracks), YouTube’s "Content ID" system will automatically detect it. What happens: The music publisher (the company that owns the rights to the hymn) will likely place a "claim" on your video. The result: They won't take the video down, but they will force ads to play on your video so they can collect the revenue. You cannot turn these ads off, even if you are a nonprofit.

3. Can you stop the ads? There is no "button" to pay YouTube to remove ads from your embedded player like there is on Vimeo. However, you can minimize the "clutter" by using a specific type of embed code. When your web developer puts the link on the site, they should use the "Modest Branding" and "No-Cookie" options. This won't 100% guarantee no ads, but it makes the player look much cleaner and more professional.

Comparison: YouTube vs. your current Vimeo setup
Feature	Vimeo (Current)	YouTube (New)
Cost	Monthly/Annual Fee	$0 (Free)
Ads	None	Possible (Pre-roll/Overlays)
Discovery	Hard to find (hidden)	High (Shows up in Google searches)
Storage	Limited by your plan	Unlimited
At the end...	Ends on a clean frame	May show "Related Videos"

My Recommendation for St. Mary's
- Since your goal is a professional, ad-free experience on your website:
- Use YouTube for Reach: Post your sermons and highlights there so people in Bonita Springs can find you via Google.
- Check your Licensing: Ensure your CCLI Streaming License number is in every video description. This doesn't always stop the ads, but it proves you have the right to play the music.
- The "Hybrid" Approach: If the church budget allows, some churches keep a "Basic" Vimeo account just for the website embed (to keep it clean) and use the free YouTube account for "social media" and discovery.

