2. Mid-Stream Ads (The "Interrupt"). The good news is that YouTube does not automatically insert "mid-roll" ads into live streams unless you (the operator) specifically tell it to. In vMix: As long as you don't click a "Trigger Ad" button in your streaming software or the YouTube Live Control Room, the service will not be interrupted once it has started. The Risk: If you play copyrighted music (like a recorded track or even some specific choir arrangements), YouTube's "Content ID" might flag the stream. While they rarely stop a live church stream for this, they might force an ad to play at the very end or on the archived version.

3. The "Archived" Experience. Once the live stream is over and becomes a "Video on Demand" (VOD) on your site: YouTube is much more likely to insert ads into these archived videos. Unlike Vimeo, which keeps these clean, YouTube treats these like any other video on their platform.

