I’ve just watched two videos from the Pawley’s Island (South Carolina) Episcopal Church. One video was over an hour long. There were no ads. How can this be?

It is understandable why that would be surprising! When you see a long church service on YouTube with zero interruptions, it usually isn’t luck—it is a combination of specific settings and how the church handles their music.

Here is how Holy Cross Faith Memorial (and other savvy churches) achieves that ad-free experience:

1. They are "Non-Monetized". Even though YouTube can show ads on any video, they generally prioritize them for channels that have "opted-in" to make money. Many churches explicitly choose not to join the YouTube Partner Program. By staying non-monetized, they aren't asking YouTube to place "Mid-roll" ads (the ones that interrupt the sermon).

2. They "Clean" Their Music. This is the biggest secret. As we discussed earlier, if a church plays a recorded CD or a specific copyrighted arrangement, YouTube’s "Content ID" system automatically places ads on the video so the record label can get paid. The Pawley's Island Strategy: They likely use "Public Domain" hymns or have a choir/organist playing original arrangements that haven't been "fingerprinted" by a major record label. If the music isn't flagged, YouTube has no "legal" obligation to force an ad onto that specific video.

3. Your Own "Viewer" Experience. There is a high probability that you didn't see ads because of your own setup: Ad-Blockers: If you are using a browser like Brave or have an extension like uBlock Origin, you won't see ads on any video, regardless of the church's settings. YouTube Premium: If you are logged into a Google account that pays for Premium, the ads are stripped away automatically. Embedded Players: Some churches use "Privacy Enhanced" links on their websites which, while not a guarantee, often result in a much cleaner, ad-lite start for the viewer.

4. The "Length" Loophole. YouTube is actually less likely to put a random ad in the middle of a 60-minute "Live Stream" than they are on a 10-minute "Viral Video." As long as the church operator doesn't manually trigger an ad break in the control room, the stream usually remains a continuous, sacred space.

What this means for St. Mary's:

- You can absolutely achieve this same result! If you:
    - Apply for the Nonprofit Program (to prove you are a church).
    - Stick to live music (choir/organ) rather than playing recorded tracks.
    - Keep "Monetization" turned OFF in your settings.

...then the vast majority of your parishioners in Bonita Springs will have the same uninterrupted experience you saw with the Pawley's Island videos.

