OK, the rector and team have met. We have decided to go with a non-profit YouTube channel. Just to be sure, with this channel there is no monetization and there are no advertisements. Is this correct? Is there anything else I should be aware of before we proceed?

That is a great decision. Choosing the Nonprofit route is the most sustainable "official" path for the church. However, there is a very important distinction to clarify with the Rector so he isn't surprised later.

1. The "No Ads" Misconception. Crucial Point: Being a "Nonprofit Channel" does not automatically make your videos ad-free for viewers. YouTube’s Ownership: YouTube is a "free" hosting service. Even for nonprofits, they reserve the right to run ads on any video to pay for their servers. The Difference: On a regular channel, you have almost no control until you are big. On a Nonprofit channel, you have the "moral authority" and better tools to manage your channel, but you still cannot simply check a box that says "Turn off all ads for everyone." Parishioner Experience: Most of your parishioners will still see a short "Pre-roll" ad before the service starts, unless they personally pay for YouTube Premium.
2. Can you avoid ads entirely?. If the Rector’s absolute priority is zero ads, YouTube is actually a difficult place to be. To truly get an ad-free experience like you have now with Vimeo, you would have to: Keep "Monetization" OFF: This tells YouTube you aren't trying to make money, which sometimes reduces ad frequency (but doesn't eliminate it). Monitor Music Claims: As we discussed, if the choir sings a copyrighted arrangement, the publisher will force ads onto your video to collect royalties. Stick to public domain hymns or original arrangements to keep the "ad-bots" away.
3. What you DO get with the Nonprofit Program. While it doesn't solve the "ad problem" 100%, the benefits are still massive: YouTube Giving: You can add a "Donate" button directly to your live streams. Link Anywhere: You can put a clickable link to the St. Mary's website inside your video—something regular channels can't do until they have thousands of subscribers. Google Ad Grants: This is the "hidden gem." You can get up to $10,000 per month in free search ads to show your church's website to people in Bonita Springs searching for things like "churches near me."

What to be aware of before you start:

- The "Goodstack" Wait: The verification through Goodstack/Percent takes 2–14 business days. Don't plan a "Grand Launch" for next Sunday until you get that approval email.
- The Master Account: Ensure the church office (not a volunteer) creates the initial Google account. You want the recovery phone number to be the church office phone.
- Permissions: Once you have the account, the first thing you should do is go to Settings > Permissions and add yourself and the Rector as "Managers." This prevents the "lockout" issue you faced before.

My Recommendation

Go ahead with the Google for Nonprofits application. It gives you the highest level of respect from Google’s support team. Just make sure the Rector knows that "Free for the Church" usually means "Ads for the Viewer" in the YouTube world.

