This decision marks a significant step toward a stable, official digital presence for St. Mary's. To ensure you and the Rector are fully prepared for the "Nonprofit" experience in 2026, here is the clear breakdown of how ads and appropriateness work.

1. Will ads always be at the beginning? Mostly, yes—if you set it up correctly. While YouTube "In-Stream" ads can technically appear at the beginning (pre-roll), middle (mid-roll), or end (post-roll), you have the power to restrict them:
- Mid-Rolls (The Interruptions): As the channel owner, you can turn off mid-roll ads for every video you upload. This ensures that once the service starts, it is never interrupted.
- Pre-Rolls (The Opening): Most ads will occur at the very beginning. This is common across YouTube. A 5–15 second ad plays, and then the viewer is taken to the stream.
- The "Holding Slide" Hack: Since you are using vMix, start your stream 5 minutes early with a "Welcome to St. Mary's" slide and quiet background music. If an ad plays, it happens while people are "arriving" in the digital pew, not during the opening hymn.
2. Will ads be appropriate for a church? By default, Google uses its standard "Family Friendly" algorithm, but you can go much further to protect the church's brand. Content Filtering: Once your nonprofit account is verified, you can access the "Ad Review Center" in your linked Google Ads account. Here, you can block specific "Sensitive Categories" that you feel are inappropriate for St. Mary's, such as: Politics & Social Issues Dating & Relationships Gambling or Alcohol Specific Competitors (e.g., you can block other denominations' ads if you wish) The Algorithm Factor: YouTube's AI tries to match the ad to the content. Because your videos will be tagged as "Religious," "Episcopal," and "Church Service," the system is naturally incentivized to show "neutral" ads like household goods, travel, or local services rather than anything shocking.
3. The Path Forward: Goodstack Verification. This is the "Golden Key" for your Parish Administrator. They need to go here to start the process: The Link: Google for Nonprofits - Get Started (The site will automatically redirect them to the Goodstack/Percent verification portal during the process.) What the Administrator needs to know: Select the "Church" category: Even if you don't have a 501(c)(3) letter (as many churches fall under group exemptions), they can use the church's EIN and a page from the official Episcopal Church Directory. The Wait: Remind them it takes 2–14 business days. The Confirmation: Once they get the "Congratulations" email, they must log back into the Google for Nonprofits portal and click "Activate" under the YouTube section.

One Final Warning for 2026

